<?php

namespace Database\Seeders;

use App\Models\AiKnowledgeBase;
use Illuminate\Database\Seeder;

class AiKnowledgeBaseSeeder extends Seeder
{
    public function run(): void
    {
        foreach ($this->records() as $record) {
            AiKnowledgeBase::updateOrCreate(
                [
                    'module' => $record['module'],
                    'title' => $record['title'],
                ],
                [
                    'content' => $record['content'],
                    'status' => AiKnowledgeBase::STATUS_ACTIVE,
                ]
            );
        }
    }

    private function records(): array
    {
        return [
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_GENERAL, 'AI Assistant Scope And Safety', 'The AI Assistant helps logged-in users understand how to use the HC Gaming trading platform. It can explain platform workflows, journal records, onboarding, trading exams, prop firm review features, and trading statistics. It must not provide buy/sell signals, market predictions, profit promises, revenge trading advice, or financial advice. If a user asks for trading advice, the assistant should redirect them to Trading Journal usage, risk review, and discipline review.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Trading Journal Overview', 'The Trading Journal module helps traders record, review, and analyze their trades. In the current system, users can record open trade time, close trade time, trading pair, direction, entry price, exit price, lot size, calculated pips, calculated profit/loss, result, and notes. The purpose is not to predict the market, but to help users learn from their own trading behavior, review risk, and build discipline.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Why Traders Should Use The Trading Journal', 'Traders should use the Trading Journal because memory is unreliable. A written record helps users see repeated mistakes, emotional patterns, overtrading, poor timing, weak risk control, and setups that do or do not match their plan. The journal should be used to improve process first, not to chase profit. Past journal results do not guarantee future profit.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'How To Add A Trading Journal Record', 'To add a trading journal record, user should go to Trader Centre > Trading Journal and click Add Trade. Then choose the time source, enter open trade time and close trade time, select a trading pair, choose Buy or Sell, enter entry price, exit price, lot size, select result, and write notes. The system calculates pips and profit/loss from the selected pair pip settings, lot size, and result. Click Save Trade after checking the details. If validation fails, check required fields and numeric values.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'How To Edit A Trading Journal Record', 'To edit a trading journal record, open the Trading Journal list, find the trade, and click the edit button. Update the necessary fields such as time, pair, direction, entry price, exit price, lot size, result, or notes, then click Update Trade. Editing is useful when the user entered wrong data, forgot notes, or wants to complete review after the trade is finished. The system checks that close time is after open time and that the update does not create a negative balance.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'How To Delete A Trading Journal Record', 'To delete a record, user should find the trade in the Trading Journal list and click Delete if the system provides this button. User should only delete wrong or duplicate records. For learning purposes, losing trades should usually not be deleted because they are important for review and improvement. Deleting records can change statistics such as win rate, profit/loss, drawdown, growth, and consistency.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Duplicate Or Multiple Same Trade Records', 'The current add-trade form includes Number of Trades, called duplicate_count in the backend. It can create multiple identical trade records from one form submission, up to 500 records. This is useful only when the user intentionally needs repeated same records. The system stops recording if the repeated trades would make the account balance negative. Users should avoid using duplicate count accidentally because it affects statistics.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Screenshots In Trading Journal', 'Screenshots are useful for reviewing trade setup, entry reason, market structure, support/resistance, order block, supply/demand, trend, and mistakes. Based on the current system information, the Trading Journal form does not show a screenshot upload field or screenshot database column. Users can write screenshot references or review notes in the Notes field for now. If screenshot upload is required, contact admin to add this feature.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Trading Pair Selection And Management', 'The Trading Journal pair dropdown uses records from the trading_pairs table. A pair has symbol, description, pip_factor, and pip_decimal. Admin or allowed users can manage trading pairs from Trading Pair pages. Pair pip settings affect pips calculation. If pips look wrong, check whether the selected pair pip_factor and pip_decimal are correct.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Trading Journal Fields Explanation', 'Open trade time is when the trade started. Close trade time is when the trade ended and must be after open time. Pair is the market/instrument such as XAUUSD or EURUSD. Direction is Buy or Sell. Entry price is where the trade was entered. Exit price is where it closed. Lot size is trade volume. Pips are calculated from entry and exit using the pair pip factor. Profit/loss is calculated from pips, lot size, and result. Result can be Win, Loss, or Break Even. Notes are used for trade reason, emotions, mistakes, lessons, setup, or psychology.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Fields Not Currently Separate Columns', 'Based on the current system information, stop loss, take profit, risk percentage, reward amount, strategy, setup, session, mistake, psychology, and screenshot upload are not separate Trading Journal fields. Users can still record these details inside the Notes field. The assistant should not tell users that these fields exist as separate inputs unless they are added later.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Win Loss And Breakeven Result', 'Result tells the system how to treat the trade outcome. Win makes calculated profit/loss positive. Loss makes calculated profit/loss negative. Break Even sets profit/loss to 0. Win rate in the journal dashboard is based on winning trades divided by winning plus losing trades, multiplied by 100. Breakeven trades are not counted in the win-rate denominator.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Risk Percentage Explanation', 'Risk percentage shows how much account capital is at risk for one trade. The current Trading Journal form does not store risk percentage as a separate field. Users can write planned risk percentage in Notes. The assistant should explain risk percentage as education only and must never encourage users to increase risk, over-leverage, or increase lot size to recover losses.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Risk Reward Ratio Explanation', 'Risk reward ratio compares planned potential loss with planned potential profit. Example: 1:2 means risking 1 unit to target 2 units. In the current system, stop loss and take profit are not separate journal fields, so exact planned R:R is not stored per trade. The dashboard risk reward value is closer to total profit divided by total loss when both exist. Risk reward does not guarantee profit; it only describes structure or historical outcome.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Win Rate Explanation', 'Win rate shows the percentage of completed trades that ended as wins. Formula in this journal dashboard: winning trades divided by winning trades plus losing trades, multiplied by 100. Breakeven trades are excluded from the denominator. A high win rate does not always mean the account is profitable if losses are much larger than wins. Users should review win rate together with average win, average loss, total profit/loss, drawdown, growth, and consistency.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Drawdown Explanation', 'Drawdown means decline from a previous account high or capital level. It helps users understand losing periods and risk pressure. A journal can help identify whether drawdown comes from poor setups, emotional trading, overtrading, revenge trading, high lot size, or poor exit discipline. Past drawdown does not guarantee future drawdown, but it is useful for risk review.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Growth Percentage Explanation', 'Growth percentage shows how much the account changed compared with deposit or starting capital. In the current journal dashboard, growth is based on net profit/loss divided by total deposits, multiplied by 100, and growth is shown as 0 when net profit/loss is not positive. If growth looks wrong, check whether capital/deposit was added, whether profit/loss is correct, whether filters are correct, and whether duplicate/deleted records changed totals.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Expectancy Score Explanation', 'Expectancy helps users understand average outcome per trade over a sample of trades. Formula used by the statistics logic is generally win probability multiplied by average win minus loss probability multiplied by average loss. Positive expectancy is healthier than negative expectancy, but it is still based on past records and does not guarantee future profit.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Consistency Score Explanation', 'Consistency score in this system includes the Best Day Rule. The system checks whether the biggest winning day is 40% or less of total generated profit. If one day creates too much of the total profit, consistency may be weak. Consistency is important because one good trade or one good day does not prove repeatable trading behavior.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Trade Quality Rating Explanation', 'Trade quality rating means whether the trade followed the plan, not only whether it won. Based on the current system information, there is no separate per-trade quality rating field in the journal form. Users can write quality notes inside Notes. A losing trade can still be high quality if it followed the plan. A winning trade can still be low quality if it was impulsive or emotional.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Capital Balance Deposit Growth And Performance', 'Capital is managed separately through the Capital module. Deposits and withdrawals affect balance and growth calculations. The journal also adds trade profit/loss to capital balance. If a user cannot add a trade, one possible reason is no available capital. If performance or growth looks wrong, check capital records, deposit amount, withdrawal records, date filters, duplicate trades, and decimal inputs.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'How To Read Trading Journal Statistics', 'Trading Journal statistics include balance, win rate, total profit, total loss, net profit/loss, risk reward, growth, drawdown, best day rule, gross profit rule, trader style, revenge tier, gambling tier, behavior penalty, layering, hedging, position consistency, average duration, expectancy, and grade. These are review indicators for discipline and performance. They should not be treated as trade signals or guarantees.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Behavioral Risk Overview', 'Behavioral risk in the Trading Journal is a discipline review feature. It helps users review whether their records show revenge trading, gambling-style behavior, or risky layering. The system calculates behavior markers from the logged-in user journal only. These markers are not trading signals, financial advice, or profit predictions. They should be used to improve process, risk control, and emotional discipline.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Revenge Trading Detection', 'Revenge trading means reacting emotionally after a loss, often trying to win back money quickly. The system detects possible revenge trading with a separate revenge_score from 0 to 100. Evidence includes last closed trade was a loss, opening during the configured cooldown after loss, opening within 5 minutes after loss, increasing risk or lot size after a loss, same-symbol pressure, same direction after a stop loss, ignoring a loss streak, daily loss limit breach, or emotional tags such as revenge, angry, panic, stress, FOMO, or greedy. Another order alone is not revenge trading. The assistant should explain this as a warning signal for review, not as a guaranteed diagnosis.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'How To Review Revenge Trading', 'When revenge trading is detected, user should review the losing trade, the next trade, delay between trades, lot size change, pair, direction, and notes. User should ask: Did I follow my plan? Did I re-enter because of emotion? Did I increase lot size to recover? Did I trade the same pair too quickly? A useful improvement is to create a cooling-off rule after losses and write the reason before entering another trade. The assistant must not tell users how to recover losses.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Gambling Trading Detection', 'Gambling-style trading means behavior that looks like betting instead of following a planned trading process. The system detects possible gambling behavior with a separate gambling_score from 0 to 100. Evidence includes missing trade plan, missing setup when not standalone, missing entry reason, missing strategy, missing stop loss, order risk above plan, setup risk above plan, overtrading, dangerous margin exposure, poor risk reward, no take profit or exit plan, or random symbols/strategy switching. Layer count, order count, holding time, and two orders are not enough by themselves. This is a journal review signal only.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'How To Review Gambling Style Behavior', 'When gambling-style behavior appears, user should review trade count per day, lot size consistency, biggest losses, rapid entries, short holding times, and whether trades were planned. The goal is to reduce impulsive decisions and build process discipline. Useful journal habits include fixed risk rules, maximum trades per day, written setup checklist, cooling-off time, and weekly behavior review. The assistant must not encourage over-leverage, bigger lot size, or revenge recovery.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Layering Strategy Detection', 'Layering in this journal means multiple same-pair, same-direction trades opened close together or while earlier trades are still open. The system still reviews layering, but planned layering should not automatically become gambling or revenge trading. A valid planned layer should have planned_layer true, setup_id, order number within max planned layers, risk within max order risk, total setup risk within max setup risk, stop loss, entry reason, strategy, safe margin, same setup symbol/direction, no cooldown breach after a loss, and daily loss below limit. Layering may be planned or risky depending on the user plan, but the assistant must not recommend layering as a buy/sell strategy.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'How To Review Layering Risk', 'When layering is detected, user should review whether every layer had a written reason, whether total lot exposure was planned, whether the layer was added while price moved against the first trade, and whether the added layer increased risk too much. Adverse layering means adding buys lower or sells higher, which can become averaging into a losing move. The journal should help users check discipline and exposure, not promise that layering will work.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Behavioral Risk Score Explanation', 'The behavioral risk score uses separate gambling_score and revenge_score values from 0 to 100. The final behaviour score is max(gambling_score, revenge_score). Layering is reviewed separately and should not automatically classify a trader as gambling or revenge trading when the layer follows the plan, risk is controlled, margin is safe, and there is no loss-chasing evidence. A low score suggests controlled behavior in the selected records. A watchlist or high score means the user should review behavior markers and journal notes carefully. This score does not predict future trades and does not guarantee profit or loss.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Behavioral Risk Troubleshooting', 'If revenge, gambling, or layering values look wrong, possible causes include missing open or close time, wrong close time order, wrong result, wrong profit/loss, incorrect lot size, duplicate records, deleted records, wrong selected month/year, missing capital for percentage-based checks, or incomplete journal sample. User should check journal records, filters, capital records, and whether duplicate_count was used accidentally.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Export Trading Journal', 'The system supports exporting the user Trading Journal to Excel through the trading-journal export route. The list page also has an export button. Export helps users review performance outside the system, share a report with a mentor, or archive monthly trading history. If export seems wrong, check filters, date range, and whether records exist.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Import Trading Journal Records', 'The system supports importing trades from xlsx, xls, or csv files with a maximum upload size of 2048 KB. Users can download a trading journal template first. If import fails, possible reasons include empty file, wrong file type, missing required columns, invalid numeric values, or validation errors in rows. Imported records are assigned to the logged-in user.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Filter And Search Journal Records', 'The Trading Journal page supports month and year filtering. Users can select a specific month/year or use all to show wider data. If records are missing, check whether the selected month/year has records and whether open trade time is inside that filter. Filters can affect statistics, export, and visible records.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'How To Review A Losing Trade', 'When reviewing a losing trade, user should check whether the trade followed the plan, whether lot size was acceptable, whether entry was emotional, whether exit was disciplined, whether market conditions changed, and what lesson should be written. The goal is improvement, not self-blame. Users should not delete losing trades just to make statistics look better.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'How To Review A Winning Trade', 'When reviewing a winning trade, user should not only focus on profit. User should check whether the trade followed the plan, whether risk and exit were disciplined, and whether the result came from good process or luck. This helps build repeatable behavior. A profitable but impulsive trade should still be marked honestly in Notes.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Common Trading Journal Mistakes', 'Common mistakes include not recording losing trades, deleting losses, entering wrong lot size, choosing the wrong trading pair, forgetting notes, writing vague notes, not reviewing emotions, using duplicate count accidentally, ignoring capital records, focusing only on profit, and not reviewing statistics regularly.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Trading Journal Best Practice', 'Users should record trades as soon as possible, include clear notes, write the reason for entry, record emotions, mark mistakes, review weekly, compare performance with trading rules, and focus on process before profit. If screenshot upload is unavailable, users can still describe the chart setup in Notes.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_JOURNAL, 'Troubleshooting Missing Or Wrong Statistics', 'If statistics are empty or wrong, possible causes include no journal records, selected date filter has no records, result or profit/loss is missing, capital/deposit is not added, pair pip factor is wrong, duplicate records exist, records were deleted, wrong decimal input, or open/close date is outside the filter. User should check Trading Journal records, capital records, pair settings, and selected filters.'),
            $this->record(AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_STATISTICS, 'Trading Statistics Meaning', 'Trading Statistics summarizes journal data using calculated numbers such as total trades, wins, losses, breakeven trades, win rate, total profit, total loss, net profit/loss, current balance, growth percentage, maximum drawdown, daily loss percentage, consistency score, expectancy, average win, average loss, risk percentage, and behavior metrics. Laravel calculates personal statistics for the logged-in user, and Gemini only explains the calculated summary in simple language.'),
        ];
    }

    private function record(string $module, string $title, string $content): array
    {
        return compact('module', 'title', 'content');
    }
}
